   126	
   127	He watched her table that evening from the loft's door, because it was a room with people in it. Nine, as she had said, on the long bench under the lamp, in the order she had put them and not the order they had come: a child with a wrist first, because a child does not wait well; then the three that mattered; then the rest. She sat down once, between the fourth and the fifth, and drank, and did not sit long. Nobody on the bench asked her for paper. Nobody asked her tier. They asked her, one at a time, to put her hands on them, and she did, and that was the whole of what she was to the steading and what the steading was to her. Thornwater fed her. He saw now what it fed her for.
