   171	The road came up out of the scrub in the afternoon and the scrub thinned as it climbed, from waist height to knee to nothing but grass and stone, so that for the first time since Thornwater he could see the road's whole length ahead of him instead of the next bend of it. It went up. Not steeply; the calf said nine on it and no more. But steadily, the grass gone short and grey-green, the ruts shallower because carts had gone up this stretch light. At the top the ground made a ridge, low, long, running across the road's line, with a shoulder to the left where the ridge fell away. And there, at the shoulder, the road did what the carter's hand had drawn. It left its line. It bent left, hard, round the shoulder, and went out of sight behind the ridge, and Karis's finger on the copy said it came back to its line a finger's width on and went east.
