   122	
   123	Lira walked at the off wheel, on the other side of the cart, and she counted nothing at all.
   124	
   125	He knew, since he could hear her. She had been told a month, and it was plain that she had taken the month as she had taken the mule, as a thing agreed to and not to be discussed. She set the great boot down on its whole sole at every step and looked at the country. There was a good deal of it to see. The road went south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, with the grey mountain shrinking behind their left shoulders, and twice a day it forded some small brown water where the mules drank below the crossing. She named nothing and timed nothing. Once, in the middle of the second afternoon, she said across the cart that she had never before walked anywhere slowly enough to see what was growing on a wall, and that it was mostly moss.
