 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 27 — The Rise
     2	
     3	Karis made her long cord along Thornwater's fence in the first grey light, with Brom to do the knots.
     4	
     5	Two of the three boxes were to stay in the loft, and so two of the three box-ropes were free. Brom married them end to end with a splice that he worked by feel, and hung the whole length from a fence-post with a stone on its end to take the stretch out, while she stood by with her short cord, the one that was knotted at every foot. When the rope had done creaking she laid the short cord along it again and again, and at each length she tied in a scrap of red wool from the hold's mending basket. Cael watched her count. She told nobody the figure, and wrote it in her notebook with her shoulder turned, as a person writes down the weight of his own purse.
     6	
     7	"It's a cord now," she said, coiling it. "It was two ropes, and they are not to be thought of as ropes again until I have done with them. Nobody is to tie a box with this."
     8	
     9	"What do I tie the box with?" said Brom.
    10	
    11	"You carry it. You have told me for a month that carrying is a different thing."
    12	
    13	The middle box stood open on the barn floor, and she had been at the others since before he woke. She had the lid off the third. He came up the ladder in time to see her lifting out the masonry chips, each in its twist of paper with a number inked on the twist, and laying them in a row on a sack; and when the last was out there was a flat canvas bag at the bottom of the box, sewn shut and sealed, with five thumb-marks in the wax.
    14	
    15	She looked at that for a breath. Then she put the lid on over it, and drove the pegs home with the heel of her hand.
    16	
    17	"Those stay," she said. "The chips come. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone that I could reach between Denvash and the Line, and a date for each, and if there is so much as a doorstep at this place, I mean to know which of them it matches."
    18	
    19	So the middle box went east on Brom's back, with the chips in it, and the lamp, and the calfskin and the printed sheet, and a quire of clean paper. He knelt and had it set on him, and passed a strap of plaited sacking over his chest, and stood up. He had refused to let anyone take a turn. Cael had seen that look on him at the edge of a moss.
    20	
    21	Oryn was waiting at the east gate with the mule.
    22	
    23	She had the two panniers on, and the hold's woman had roped a roll of six grey blankets across the pack, behind the long shape in its oiled cover, with instructions that they were to come back. The healer stood on the mule's far side from the hill. She had slept, by the look of her, and she gave the five of them one glance from her stride and a half, each in turn, and seemed to find nothing in it that wanted her hands.
    24	
    25	"You'll walk at her pace," she said. "It's the only one I've got."
    26	
