   255	He was a pace short of the line. He walked.
   256	
   257	He had been certain, in the part of himself that kept accounts, of what would happen: a thing taken out of a Path would stop where Paths stopped. He had supposed it since a plank, as the others supposed it, and for the same reason, which was that no other sum came out even; and he had braced for the read to go from him as a count had gone from a healer's lips.
   258	
   259	It ran across the stride without a break.
   260	
   261	The turf under his left boot was outside the line, and the turf under his right was within it, and he had the weight of both as he had the weight of his own hands. Nothing dimmed. He took another step, and a third, and the ground went on telling him what it was.
   262	
   263	He stood still, three strides inside, with his heart going.
   264	
   265	Then he did the second thing. He had not been sure, a minute ago, that he would.
   266	
   267	He walked in, as a man goes who means to look at the ground that he will presently lead people over, without haste, twenty strides toward the ring. A grey boulder lay there in the grass, as high as his hip, and he walked straight at it. He kept his face to the east; if anyone at the stone had glanced his way, there was only a person with a notebook going to see what lay beyond a rock.
   268	
   269	At the twentieth stride he let Wind have his feet.
   270	
   271	It was a long way short of a burst. It was the smallest piece of what Lira's fighting had taught him, the lean that takes a body off a line a hair before the blow arrives; and he asked it for a foot. His weight went over without his deciding it. The boulder, which had been in front of him, was on his right hand, and he was past it, and the grass had hardly stirred.
   272	
   273	Anybody watching from behind would have seen a person walk round a rock, and no more.
   274	
   275	He stood on the far side with the tape drawn tight across his ribs and something going out of him into the cold air, small, as a breath goes out. He listened, and there was the wind, and the hiss of seed-heads, and far off toward the east a bird that got up and settled again. Nothing whatever came through the grass.
   276	
   277	He shut them both. He put Wind down, and closed the read as one closes a book, and they went back to where the other seven lay.
   278	
   279	He did not know why. He set that beside a healer's word from a hollow of shale, and added nothing to it, as she had added nothing. Five people had lost what they had at that stone, each in a different way, and one of them had asked with her whole body and been given a step. And twenty strides inside it, the things that he carried had answered him as they answered him anywhere.
   280	
   281	He walked back along the line, and stepped out over the first turf, and came down to the stone with the leaf in his hand.
   282	
   283	Karis took it from him, and read down the five rows, nodding at each. Then she laid it on her knee, and took the pencil, and ruled a sixth.
   284	
   285	"And yours?"
   286	
   287	She said it to the leaf. It was a clerk's question; she had a table with a row empty.
   288	
   289	He had perhaps a breath. He saw Lira, stamping her foot down into a boot the size of a bucket, with her face still stripped from that one step, and he saw a healer flexing her fingers; and he saw that the healer was the plainest reason of all, since she knew nothing of what he was. He could not have said, at that stone, in front of her, that the things he carried had answered him in there, without telling her what he carried; and that was not his alone to tell.
   290	
   291	It was a real reason. He distrusted it for coming so readily to hand.
   292	
   293	"It was dark before," said Cael. "It's dark now."
   294	
   295	Both halves were true. He had looked at that place going over and coming back, and it had been the same dark. Karis wrote. He watched her pencil put the two halves down, one after the other, and he knew what she would take them to mean, and what the other four would take them to mean when they read her leaf; and that the whole was not the truth, though there was not a false word in it.
   296	
   297	It was the first thing of that kind that he had kept from any of them in a long while, and he had done it at a stone, at noon, with a pencil going.
   298	
   299	"The sun's over," said Lira. She was looking up, with her hand shading her eyes. "If we're going, it's now."

