     1	# Chapter 30 — The Far Lip
     2	
     3	Six of them went over the stone a little after noon, and the mule stayed where she was.
     4	
     5	Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
     6	
     7	They took very little. Brom had the middle box on his back, with the chips in it and the paper, since whatever a surveyor had left would want carrying home in something. Karis had the long cord and the short one. There was the hedging bill, and Lira's knife, and Seln's, and a skin of water. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
     8	
     9	"East, along the old line of the road," said Karis. "I want the cord laid from this stone to the middle, a length at a time, as we laid it round the edge. If I have my sum right, the tenth will end among those stones. If I have it wrong, I should like to be told so by the ground and not by a table."
    10	
    11	So they went in as they had gone round. Brom held one end at the stone, and Karis walked the other out until it came tight, and stopped; then he came up to her, and she went on. The other four walked behind, in file, in the track that the cord left. The grass stood to Cael's hip. It closed over each of them as they passed and did not quite rise again, and when he looked back from the third length there was a lane trodden through it, as straight as a ruled margin, with a grey stone and a small brown mule at the far end.
    12	
    13	He had his nine fragments as he always had them, and he held them. Wind leaned, since Lira was in front of him. Anchor had fallen quiet at the stake and had not asked since. He had opened nothing after a boulder, and he did not open anything now; but he walked those two hundred yards as the only one of the six who knew that he could have, and it was a strange thing to carry at the back of a file.
    14	
    15	At the eighth length the ground began to rise, gently, and the grass grew shorter. At the ninth it was at his knee, and he could see the whole of what stood on the swell in front of them.
    16	
    17	It was a floor, and it was round.
    18	
    19	It lay on the crown of the rise like the lid of a great jar, grey and pale and quite bare, and the short turf came up to its edge and stopped there as cleanly as water stops at the side of a bowl. Round its rim, a stride in from the edge, stood the eleven stones. He had counted them from three hundred yards and had the number right. They were grey, like the floor, and squared, each as thick through as a man's body. Not one of them was whole. The tallest stood twice Brom's height before it ended, and its neighbour came no higher than a knee, and between those two there was every height that a broken thing can have.
    20	
    21	"Tenth," said Karis.
    22	
    23	She had gone up onto the floor with the cord's end in her hand. It came tight while she was still walking, and she stopped where it stopped her. She was standing at the middle of the round, as near as his eye could tell, with the eleven stones in a ring about her at an equal distance.
    24	
    25	She did not say anything about her sum. She laid the end of the cord down very carefully on the grey stone, and set her notebook on it for a weight, and then she paced from where she stood to the rim, in four directions, counting. The four counts came out within a stride of each other.
    26	
    27	"Ten cords," she said at last. Her voice was not altogether steady. "I worked it at the stone from the round, with a pencil. And I have walked it now with a rope, from the edge, in a straight line, and there is not a full stride between the two. I have never in my life had a figure come home like that."
    28	
    29	Nobody had stepped onto the floor after her. Five people stood on the turf at its western edge, in the track of the cord, and looked.
    30	
    31	---
    32	
    33	The edge was the first thing that he catalogued, since it was the first thing that his boots came to.
    34	
    35	The floor had a lip. All the way round, as far as he could see to either hand, its rim turned up and over in a smooth roll, like the rim of a dish, so that the edge of it stood a hand above the turf outside and a finger above the floor within. He set his foot on it and stepped up. It was a small step. It was enough that a person running would have had to think about it.
    36	
    37	Something stirred in him at that rolled edge and asked its one question, as it asked of every arris and sill, and for once Cael could not have answered it. Blade wanted to know whether the thing was an edge. He looked at a rim that curved over like the lip of a bowl, without a corner anywhere on it, and he did not know.
    38	
    39	Then he was on the floor, and he stood still.
    40	
    41	It was colder than the turf. He felt that through his soles. It was level, so far as his feet could judge, and it gave back no sound at all; Lira had come up beside him in one boot of her own and one the size of a bucket, and neither of them rang. The low sun lay across it from the south-west and threw the shadows of eleven stones in long bars to the far rim. Between the bars the grey shone a little, dully, as a slate shines that has been wetted.
    42	
    43	"Pace it," said Karis. "Rim to rim, through where the cord ends. I want yours, since yours are yards."
    44	
    45	He paced it from the western lip to the eastern, past her notebook, and had twenty-six strides. He paced it again across the other way, from south to north, and had twenty-six.
    46	
    47	"It is level under the cord," she said, when he came back.
    48	
    49	She had been busy. She and Brom had drawn the long cord tight across the whole width, lip to lip, a hand above the stone, and she had gone along beneath it on her knees with the short cord, measuring the gap. Now she had the brass straightedge out of the box, with its pebble on a thread, and was laying it toward each side of the floor in turn.
    50	
    51	"The same gap from one side to the other, to the thickness of a knot. And no pitch. I have laid this brass on a mountain for seven days, and on every hundred of it the thread hung to one side by so many hands. Here it hangs on its mark whichever way I point it." She sat back on her heels. "A threshing floor is not as flat as this. A table is not."
    52	
    53	"What is it?" said Brom. He had let the box down and was crouched beside it, with one big hand laid on the grey. "It's not slab. Slab's got a grain. It's not laid, either; I can't find a course."
    54	
    55	"I am about to try to tell you."
    56	
    57	She had the lid off the middle box. The masonry chips lay in their row, each in its twist of paper with a number inked on it, and she began to take them out, one at a time, and to hold each against the floor in the slanting light. Cael watched the first dozen. There was a honey-coloured stone that he knew from the walls of Ostrand, and a dark close one from a district hall, and a white chip with a sparkle in it that had come off a bridge. She laid every one of them down on the grey and looked from the chip to the floor and back, and put it away.
    58	
    59	It took her a quarter of an hour, and she had more than forty.
    60	
    61	"None," she said. She was very pale. "It is not the colour of any of them, or the grain. It has no grain. I have every kind of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in that box, and a date for each, and this matches not one."
    62	
    63	He had knelt down a little way off while she worked.
    64	
    65	He put both palms on the floor. It was smooth, and cold, and very slightly rough under the skin, like the shell of an egg. He moved his hands outward over it as far as his ribs would let him, feeling for the thing that every floor he had ever knelt on possessed somewhere: a crack, or a line where one piece met the next.
    66	
    67	His fingers found none. And he knew that fingers could be wrong, on a surface so close, and that the others were all looking at a box of chips.
    68	
    69	He opened the read.
    70	
    71	It was the same thing that he had done at the line, done now for work, and he did not count it a third. For a pace and a half round his knees, and a pace and a half straight down, he had the weight of what he was kneeling on. It was one thing. There was no bed under it, and no joint through it, and no place where it changed. An upright stood within his reach, the short one, and he took in the foot of that as well. It went down into the floor as a horn goes into a skull. He could find no line between them.
    72	
    73	He closed it again. Nothing had come through the grass.
    74	
    75	He had known since a hill of plates what he thought of a reading that nobody else could check, and it had not altered. So he took the pencil and wrote in Karis's table, under her *level*, the only part of what he knew that any one of them could have got with a hand and a pair of eyes.
    76	
    77	*No seam.*
    78	
    79	"Nor at the foot of the stones?" said Karis. She had read it upside down.
    80	
    81	"Not that I can find."
    82	
    83	"Then I shall write that two of us looked." She put it down. "It is a poor sort of catalogue. *Round. Level. No seam. Matches nothing.* I have written four things about it and three of them are that something is absent."
    84	
    85	Lira had wandered to the nearest of the tall stones, and she was standing in front of it with her head tipped back.
    86	
    87	"There's writing on it," she said.
    88	
    89	They went to see. It was not on that one alone. Round every upright, at the height of a person's chest, there ran a band a hand wide; and in the band there were lines. They were sunk in the grey to the depth of a fingernail, fine and even, some straight and some hooked, some crossing others at a slant. They went right round the stone and met themselves. The same band ran along the inner face of the lip, low down, all the way about the floor. Cael looked for a letter in it, and found none that he knew, and he could not have sworn that they were letters at all.
    90	
    91	"Whose hand's that?" said Lira. "You've got every sort of alphabet there is, in those boxes. You copied half of them out yourself."
    92	
    93	Karis had her nose a hand's breadth from the band. She went the whole way round the stone before she answered, and when she came back to them she was not pale any longer. She had two spots of colour high on her cheeks.
    94	
    95	"None I own. And I own more than the registry prints."
    96	
    97	She was already reaching for the paper.
    98	
    99	It took the rest of the afternoon. She would not copy by eye, since she said that an eye puts in what it expects to see; she laid a clean sheet against the band, and had Lira hold it flat with both hands, and rubbed the side of her pencil over it until the lines came up white out of the grey. She took a sheet to a stone. She numbered each in its corner, and wrote beside the number how high the stone stood, and which way it faced, and Brom carried them to the box, one at a time, on the flat of his two hands, as he would have carried a tray of eggs.
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	"There is nothing here," said Seln.
   104	
   105	He had let them work. Cael had seen him go about the floor, in no hurry, with his hands behind him and his eyes down; he had been to the foot of each of the eleven stones, and all round the outside of the lip, and twice he had gone off into the grass and come back. Now he stood at the middle, by the cord's end.
   106	
   107	"I have been looking for what we were sent to fetch. A case, I supposed, or a tripod, or a chain. A peg. The mark where a peg had been." He turned one hand over. "There is nothing on this floor but ourselves, and nothing at the foot of any stone. I have walked a round in the grass at twenty yards from the lip, and another at forty. I find our own track, coming in from the west. I find nothing that a person has set down, or dropped, or driven."
   108	
   109	"It's been five years," said Brom. "By your stake."
   110	
   111	"It has. Leather would be gone, and wood. Brass would not, nor iron. And I should expect to see where a thing had lain, on turf that has never been grazed." He looked at Cael. "I do not say that nothing was ever left. I say that I cannot find it, and that I have looked as well as I know how."
   112	
   113	Cael got up off his knees.
   114	
   115	He had expected a good many things of this place, and that had not been among them. He stood on a grey floor with the paper's second sentence going through his head: instruments, and abandoned, and a site. Somebody had sat at a ruled desk and written that, with the sum in words and again in a bracket, and had sent it over a river with a hundred marks in a bag. It had lain a month under a stone. A captain had read it twice and put it back.
   116	
   117	There were no instruments. There was a stake on the line with a registry's stamp on it, and that was the only thing within a quarter of a mile that a surveyor had ever touched.
   118	
   119	"He said he'd write what was done," said Lira slowly. She had her two hands spread on a sheet of paper against the ninth stone. "The old man. If the clerk's mark never goes on it, the box stays shut."
   120	
   121	"Then it stays shut," said Brom. "We've walked four days for a line."
   122	
   123	"For eleven sheets and a line," said Karis, without lifting her pencil. "I should not undersell the sheets."
   124	
   125	Cael looked at the sun.
   126	
   127	It had come round into the west while they worked, and it stood now above the hills that they had crossed two days ago. He put his arm out straight and laid his hand along the skyline, fingers together, and then his other hand on top of that. The lower edge of the sun sat on his upper knuckle.
   128	
   129	Two hands. He had said it at the eastern runs that morning, with a print in front of him, and Seln had said that he would hold him to it.
   130	
   131	"Two stones more," said Karis. She had seen what he was doing. "The tenth and the eleventh. A quarter of an hour, if Lira doesn't sneeze. I shall never stand here again; you heard what he said about going in twice."
   132	
   133	He weighed it. He had set a limit, and he knew what a limit was worth that gave by a quarter of an hour the first time it was leaned on. He also knew that nine sheets out of eleven was a thing she would carry for the rest of her life. And the hillside was as empty as it had been at noon. No bird had got up. Nothing had stirred the grass.
   134	
   135	"The two," he said. "No more. Seln, go round the far side once, by the east, and see that we've missed nothing under the lip. When you're back, we're in that lane and walking."
   136	
   137	"You said two hands," said Seln mildly.
   138	
   139	"I did. I'm spending a quarter of an hour that I told you I wouldn't, and I want it entered against me."
   140	
   141	Seln gave the small bow that he gave to an order he disliked, and went.
   142	
   143	Oryn had sat down on the western lip with her boot off. She had watched the whole afternoon from there, pressing the strip on her heel now and then with her thumb, and she had not once asked what any of it was for.
   144	
   145	"It's a queer thing," she said, to nobody. "I've been on a great many floors with people on them. This is the first where I couldn't have told you which of you was tired."
   146	
   147	"I feel thirteen," said Lira.
   148	
   149	She had said it lightly, with her hands on the tenth sheet. Then she seemed to hear it herself.
