   151	"I do, though. That's what it's like. The year before my Kindling I used to run the lanes at Fenmark against the big girls, with nothing in me, just legs, and I'd forgotten there was ever such a person." She shifted her palm for the pencil. "I didn't think that I'd meet her again. I'm not sure I mind."
   152	
   153	---
   154	
   155	Seln did not come back round.
   156	
   157	Cael saw him stop. He had gone along the outside of the lip, on the turf, past the south and on toward the east, where the tallest of the stones threw its bar of shadow off the floor's edge and down the slope beyond. He was a narrow dark figure at the far rim. And he had stopped as a man stops who has put his hand on a hot dish: all at once, with every part of him.
   158	
   159	He did not call. He lifted his left hand a little from his side, with the fingers spread, and kept it there.
   160	
   161	"Leave that," said Cael.
   162	
   163	Karis had the eleventh sheet half rubbed. She looked up at his face, and took the paper off the stone as it was.
   164	
   165	They crossed the floor together, the five of them, twenty-six strides, and their boots were silent on it. At the eastern lip Cael stepped down onto the turf beside Seln and saw what he was looking at.
   166	
   167	The ground had gone from under the rim.
   168	
   169	On every other side the turf came up to the floor's edge and stopped a hand below it. Here the slope fell away to the east more steeply, and at some time the earth beneath the lip had slumped and been carried off, so that the rolled edge of the grey stone stood out over nothing, like the eave of a roof. Under it there was a hollow. It ran back beneath the floor farther than he could see, low and dark, with a mouth as long as a cart and as high as his thigh.
   170	
   171	The earth in front of that mouth was bare, and it was trodden as hard as a threshing floor.
   172	
   173	He looked at it a piece at a time, as he looked at everything. There were prints. They lay one upon another, hundreds deep, so that it was only at the edges that he could tell a single foot: as broad as a palm, without a claw. The freshest had broken the morning's frost, and their rims were still sharp. To one side there was a heap of bones, deer for the most part, cracked and grey. There was scat, old and new. Tufts of coarse hair had caught on the underside of the stone lip, at the height of a low grey back. And just within the mouth, where the daylight still reached, there was a place in the bare earth the length of a hound, scooped and worn as smooth as the seat of an old chair.
   174	
   175	There were a good many such places. He could see the first four. They went back into the dark in a row.
   176	
   177	Nothing was in them. He checked that twice. The hollow was empty, as far as light went, and no sound came out of it, and the wind brought him only the smell of cold earth and something rank and dry.
   178	
   179	Seln had gone down on one knee at the edge of the trodden ground. He had not touched it. He was reading, with his head a little on one side, as Cael had seen him read a wet margin beside a pool on a mountain.
   180	
   181	"This morning," he said, very low. "Before we had laid the first cord. More than six. I should say eight, or nine; they have gone over each other's marks and I cannot do better." He pointed, without lifting his hand from his knee, at the newest prints. They led away from the mouth, down the slope, to the east. "Out. All of them, and none back since."
   182	
   183	"Then where are they?" said Lira. It was hardly a breath.
   184	
   185	"I do not know. I have seen no animal on this hillside since the sun rose, and I have been looking for nothing else."
   186	
   187	Brom had come up on Cael's other side, with the box on his back. He was looking at the bones.
   188	
   189	"Why here?" he said. "There's a thousand hollows in a country like this. There's a whole mountain two days west with caves in it. Why would a thing with teeth choose to lie under the one floor that the carters won't walk on?"
   190	
   191	Seln did not turn his head.
   192	
   193	"They live in here because nothing that walks in can fight."
   194	
   195	It lay on the cold air. Cael heard it, and so did the rest, and he watched it reach each of them in turn.
   196	
   197	A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
   198	
   199	Lira was looking west.
   200	
   201	They all were. He saw their heads turn, one after another, across twenty-six strides of grey floor and the eleven stones, to the lane that the cord had left in the grass. It ran away from them down the slope, straight, pale in the low light. The grey stone at its end was too small to see. He knew where it was. From the middle of the floor it lay two hundred and twenty yards to the west, and they were standing on the wrong side of the floor from it.
   202	
   203	Then they looked at the sun.
   204	
   205	It was on the ridge. He had measured two hands under it a quarter of an hour ago, and spent the quarter of an hour, and the lower rim of it was touching the hills now, red and flattened, and going. The shadows of the eleven stones had run out across the floor and joined. Between them, where the last light struck it level, the grey had begun to shine.
   206	
   207	Then four of them looked at Oryn.
   208	
   209	She stood a little apart, with her broad hands hanging open. She had told them at noon what they had in her, on this side of a stone, and she met their eyes one after another and did not pretend that it had altered.
   210	
   211	And then, all five, they looked at him.
   212	
   213	He had the ruling. He was the nearest thing that they had to a captain, and it was his to say which way and how fast. That was the whole of what was in those five faces. Not one of them was asking him anything else.
   214	
   215	He stood with it. *Nothing that walks in can fight.* It was true of five of the six. He had known since a boulder that it was not true of the sixth, and he had told nobody, and he was the person they were all waiting on.
   216	
   217	---
   218	
   219	He wrote it there, at the far lip, standing, with the book against his forearm.
   220	
   221	It took him less time than it took Brom to shift the box higher on his shoulders and draw the strap tight. He had always written at the start of a thing. He did not know how to begin one without it, and he thought that this might be the last quiet minute of the day.
   222	
   223	*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
   224	
   225	*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
   226	
   227	He looked at what he had put.
   228	
   229	There was a line that came next. He had written it at the foot of every entry since a shelf above a valley with no name, and for the last forty-three days it had ended in the same word. His pencil went to the place.
   230	
   231	He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
   232	
